---
import ArticleSchema from "../components/ArticleSchema.astro";
import AuthorByline from "../components/AuthorByline.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  fullTitle="Shanghai Restaurant Prices: What to Expect in 2026"
  description="Complete guide to Shanghai restaurant prices. From budget street food to Michelin-starred fine dining. Understand what you will pay at every level."
>
  <main>
    <section class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pt-20 md:pt-28 pb-12">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl tracking-tighter leading-none font-semibold text-slate-900">Shanghai Restaurant Prices Guide</h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-slate-500 mt-6 leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">From RMB 20 street food to RMB 2,000 tasting menus, Shanghai dining spans every price point. Here is what to expect at each level.</p>
        <AuthorByline />
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
      <div class="space-y-8 max-w-3xl">
        <div class="card-soft bg-white shadow-tinted p-6 flex gap-4">
          <span class="text-3xl shrink-0">&#x1f4b0;</span>
          <div><h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Budget: RMB 20-80 per person</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mt-1 leading-relaxed">Street food stalls, dumpling shops, and noodle houses. This is where you find the most authentic flavors. Xiaolongbao for RMB 28, a bowl of noodles for RMB 18.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="card-soft bg-white shadow-tinted p-6 flex gap-4">
          <span class="text-3xl shrink-0">&#x1f4b8;</span>
          <div><h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Moderate: RMB 80-250 per person</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mt-1 leading-relaxed">Casual sit-down restaurants and popular chains. Full meals with multiple dishes. Most of Shanghai's beloved mid-range spots fall here.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="card-soft bg-white shadow-tinted p-6 flex gap-4">
          <span class="text-3xl shrink-0">&#x2728;</span>
          <div><h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Premium: RMB 250-800 per person</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mt-1 leading-relaxed">Upscale dining, private rooms, and exceptional service. Tasting menus and premium ingredients like hairy crab drive the price higher.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="card-soft bg-white shadow-tinted p-6 flex gap-4">
          <span class="text-3xl shrink-0">&#x2B50;</span>
          <div><h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Michelin & Fine Dining: RMB 800+ per person</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mt-1 leading-relaxed">World-class tasting menus, wine pairings, and unforgettable experiences. Fu He Hui, Xin Rong Ji, and UltraViolet represent the pinnacle of the city's <a href="https://guide.michelin.com/en/cn/shanghai-municipality/shanghai/restaurants" rel="noreferrer">MICHELIN Guide</a> scene.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="max-w-3xl mt-12 space-y-6 text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900">What Actually Drives the Bill</h2>
        <p>The same meal can double in price depending on a handful of factors. Location is the biggest: a noodle bowl that costs RMB 18 in a residential lane commands RMB 40-plus in a Jing'an mall or on the Bund. Ingredients move it next — a dish built around in-season hairy crab or wild-caught river shrimp costs far more than the same preparation with farmed protein. Finally, whether the room takes a service charge and how much alcohol you order can quietly add a third to the total before you ever taste a thing.</p>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Tipping, Tax and How You Pay</h2>
        <p>Prices shown on menus are final — there is no sales tax added at the register, and no tipping culture. A handful of high-end and hotel restaurants add a 10–15 percent service charge, which is stated on the menu. Payment is overwhelmingly digital: WeChat Pay and Alipay are accepted nearly everywhere, including street stalls, while international credit cards work in mid-range and upscale venues but not at small shops. Their grip on that digital shift is near-total — <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WeChat_Pay" rel="noreferrer">Wikipedia</a> notes the two apps "combined ... account for around 90% of China's mobile payment market." Carrying a small amount of cash and having Alipay linked to your card before you fly is the safest setup.</p>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Is Shanghai Dining Good Value?</h2>
        <p>By the standards of London, New York, or Tokyo, yes — emphatically. A memorable multi-course Benbang dinner with wine for two often lands where a single casual mains-and-a-drink meal would abroad, and everyday eating — dumplings, noodles, congee — remains inexpensive. The exceptions are the tasting-menu tier and imported items like cheese, coffee, and Western fine dining, which price on par with any global city. For most travelers, the sweet spot is mid-range sit-down Chinese: exceptional quality at RMB 80–250 a head.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10">
        <a href="/" class="btn-soft bg-accent-600 hover:bg-accent-700 text-white text-sm font-medium px-6 py-3 transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2">Explore Restaurants by Budget</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <ArticleSchema
    headline="Shanghai Restaurant Prices Guide"
    description="Complete guide to Shanghai restaurant prices"
    url="https://www.shanghai-restaurant.com/shanghai-restaurant-prices"
  />
</Layout>
